<% content_for :title, "Small Pharmacy DSCSA Exemption Extended to #{DscsaDeadline.label} — What Still Applies Now" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "The FDA extended the DSCSA small dispenser exemption to #{DscsaDeadline.label}. Who qualifies, what's still required today, and how to use the extra year." %>

<% content_for :full_bleed, "1" %>
<div class="doc-prose mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14">
  <h1>Small Pharmacy DSCSA Exemption Extended to <%= DscsaDeadline.label %> — What Still Applies Now</h1>
  <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-faint">Last updated: <%= Date.current.strftime("%B %-d, %Y") %></p>

  <% if DscsaDeadline.passed? %>
    <p class="rounded border p-3 text-[14px]" style="background-color:#fbf1e4;border-color:#ecd3ac;color:#5c4420;">
      Update: this date has now passed. The exemption ended on <strong><%= DscsaDeadline.label %></strong>,
      and the enhanced requirements below apply to small dispensers today.
      <%= link_to "Check where your pharmacy stands →", dscsa_assessment_path, class: "underline" %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <p>
    The FDA&rsquo;s small dispenser exemption from the Drug Supply Chain Security Act (DSCSA) was
    due to end on November 27, 2026. On <strong><%= DscsaDeadline.extended_label %></strong>, the FDA
    extended it by a year, to <strong><%= DscsaDeadline.label %></strong>, while it assesses whether
    small pharmacies can feasibly do electronic, package-level tracing. That&rsquo;s a reprieve on the
    enhanced requirements, not on DSCSA as a whole. This is a plain-language summary &mdash; not legal
    advice (see the disclaimer at the bottom).
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">What is the small dispenser exemption?</h2>
    <p>
      DSCSA&rsquo;s enhanced drug distribution security (EDDS) requirements &mdash; electronic,
      package-level tracing of prescription drugs and interoperable data exchange between trading
      partners &mdash; took effect for most of the supply chain on November 27, 2023. The FDA gave
      smaller pharmacies more time, first through November 27, 2026 and now through
      <%= DscsaDeadline.label %>. The exemption is automatic: there&rsquo;s nothing to file or apply for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Who qualifies as a &ldquo;small dispenser&rdquo;</h2>
    <p>
      A small dispenser is a pharmacy whose owning corporate entity employs <strong>25 or fewer
      full-time licensed pharmacists or qualified pharmacy technicians</strong> in total, across all
      of its locations. Under the extension, that count is measured as of
      <strong><%= DscsaDeadline.qualifying_label %></strong>. Edge cases &mdash; a pharmacy opening
      after that date, a change in ownership &mdash; aren&rsquo;t fully addressed, so if your situation
      isn&rsquo;t clear-cut, confirm with counsel or your pharmacy association rather than assuming.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">What still applies today</h2>
    <p>
      The extension covers specific enhanced requirements. The longstanding DSCSA obligations that
      predate them still apply to small dispensers now, including:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>Buying from and selling to authorized trading partners only</li>
      <li>Accepting product only when it carries the required product identifier</li>
      <li>Quarantining and investigating suspect product, and notifying the FDA and trading partners
        when a product is determined to be illegitimate</li>
      <li>Keeping transaction records for six years and producing them on request</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">What changes on <%= DscsaDeadline.label %></h2>
    <p>Once the exemption ends, small dispensers take on the enhanced obligations everyone else already has:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>Receiving and maintaining electronic, package-level transaction data from trading partners
        for every product received</li>
      <li>Verifying product identifiers and tracing individual packages, not just lots</li>
      <li>Responding to verification and tracing requests at the package level</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The FDA is assessing feasibility for small dispensers, so details could shift again. Planning
      for the current date is the safe assumption.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">How to use the extra year</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>Confirm your headcount as of <%= DscsaDeadline.qualifying_label %>, in writing, from whoever
        tracks it</li>
      <li>Ask each wholesaler how they send electronic transaction data, and whether you can receive
        and store it</li>
      <li>Write down your suspect-product process now &mdash; it&rsquo;s required today, not next year</li>
      <li>Get custody and cold-chain records into a system you could hand an inspector, so the
        November 2027 work is an upgrade rather than a rebuild</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2">Where this fits with the transport side</h2>
    <p>
      Pharma Transport doesn&rsquo;t replace your wholesaler&rsquo;s serialization system &mdash; but
      the custody-event logging and compliance packets it produces for each shipment give you an
      audit-ready record of what happened in transit and at delivery. If that&rsquo;s useful to see,
      <%= link_to "you can sign up and try it", new_user_registration_path %>, or
      <%= link_to "take the free readiness check", dscsa_assessment_path %> first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 border-t pt-4">
    This page is general information based on FDA guidance available as of the date above, not
    legal advice. DSCSA dates have shifted before &mdash; including this one &mdash; and could again.
    Confirm current requirements against the FDA&rsquo;s own DSCSA exemptions page or with counsel
    before relying on this for compliance decisions.
  </p>
</div>
